        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Interesting! In the popup it actually ran through and opened https://github.com/MandavkarPranjal/environment — wait, that's the real repo of this working directory? The popup started in /tmp/opencode/repro... but the URL is the environment repo. Hmm — because `tmux display-popup` without -t targets active session \"environment\" whose pane cwd... no, I passed -d /tmp/opencode/repro. But the script's TMUX block does `cd $(tmux display-message -p '#{pane_current_path}')` which returns the ACTIVE PANE's path = the user's actual pane in ~/dev/environment! So popup opened the environment repo. Anyway, it completed — not stuck here.\n\nSo where's the stuck case? Maybe when run via `run-shell`, or maybe the user's complaint: pressing prefix+g opens popup and it works... but they say stuck. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the issue: when there are MULTIPLE fzf prompts and the first one exits with 130 or empty (Esc), fine.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe user runs `open-repo` from within a shell inside tmux (not popup). Then TMUX block cds to pane_current_path (same dir anyway) and fzf works as my repro showed. Not stuck.\n\nMaybe \"stuck\" happens when tmux display-message fails? Or when the browser launch inside popup: systemd-run --user ... nohup & — popup closes on exit; background process should survive.\n\nHmm wait — maybe stuck = popup never closes / hangs after selection? My test completed in 4s without interaction though — how did fzf get answered?! The log shows \"Opening: ...\" meaning both fzf prompts were answered. Did the user interact?? Or did fzf read EOF? With 2 remotes, remote_count=2 so first fzf ran... and got a selection somehow. Unless fzf errored quickly. Actually maybe the attached client saw popups flash. Possibly the USER (himmel) pressed keys! It's their live session. Oops.\n\nActually more likely: fzf exited immediately due to error and selected empty... but then destination would be empty → exit 0 without \"Opening\". We got a URL, so both fzf calls returned selections. Someone answered them, OR fzf auto-picked due to immediate EOF? No—EOF gives empty selection → exit 0.\n\nHmm wait, second fzf has 6 choices; \"GitHub\" chosen presumably. First fzf picked one of two remotes. That required real keypresses. So the user at pts/0 interacted! Funny. OK so popup works interactively too...\n\nUnless... the user pressed keys trying to unstick it. So maybe the popup DID open and was stuck/blank for them, and they mashed keys.\n\nKey insight from my test: the TMUX block cd'd to the wrong directory (active pane path instead of popup start dir). But that's a correctness bug, not stuck.\n\nLet me reconsider \"stuck\": Perhaps when running through tmux, fzf's `--height=40%` inside a popup conflicts. Known issue: fzf --height uses alternate screen; in tmux popup this can render nothing until resize... There IS a known junegunn/fzf issue about popups: fzf #3536? I recall fzf-tmux added `-p` popup support and docs say plain fzf doesn't work well in popups? Let me search web to confirm known behavior.",
