oes zen open when running flatpak run directly vs through uwsm-app?\n\nThe uwsm-app daemon logs show it forwards to systemd-run --scope, which starts flatpak, which fails with EPERM clone after ~500ms. If from shell the user instead gets... hmm, but the script logic doesn't depend on shell-vs-tmux except TMUX var. Branches identical.\n\nUNLESS: from shell, the script ALSO fails the same way but zen is ALREADY RUNNING → flatpak run just activates existing instance (no sandbox spawn needed?) Actually flatpak run always spawns. Hmm, but if Zen is already running, `flatpak run app.zen_browser.zen URL` sends URL to existing instance via D-Bus and exits quickly — success regardless. When Zen is NOT running, spawning fails with EPERM?\n\nWait but user says shell→opens good. Maybe from shell they tested while zen already running.\n\nHmm, alternatively the real difference: environment variables. From interactive shell (inside Hyprland session), WAYLAND_DISPLAY etc. present; uwsm-app's inner systemd-run --scope inherits and passes them → flatpak child can connect to Wayland. Via outer systemd-run --user unit (transient unit), the environment comes from the USER MANAGER, not the calling shell! If WAYLAND_DISPLAY isn't imported into the user manager (systemctl --user import-environment), the flatpak app can't reach the compositor → exits immediately (~500ms, 104M memory peak = it started rendering then died).\n\nBut uwsm-app re-executes via `systemd-run --user --scope` — --scope runs in the CALLER's context? No: --scope means systemd creates a scope around a process ALREADY forked by the caller (uwsm_app-daemon?). Look at log: \"uwsm_app-daemon sent: exec systemd-run --user --scope ...\" — there's a uwsm app DAEMON involved. The daemon process was presumably started long ago from the session with full env. The daemon receives request and execs systemd-run --scope itself → inherits DAEMON's env. If daemon has Wayland env, fine.\n\nThe EPERM clone error: \"[2] Sandbox: CanCreateU