        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "No journal entries in the last minute for systemd-run/uwsm! The script traced up to `date +%s%N` but the `systemd-run` line never appears in the journal — meaning `nohup systemd-run ... &` was backgrounded and then... the popup closed immediately, killing the not-yet-executed child?\n\nRace condition: the script backgrounds systemd-run with `&`, script exits instantly, display-popup -E closes the popup and **kills the pane's process group**. nohup only protects against SIGHUP; but tmux popup closing kills children how? When popup closes, tmux sends SIGHUP to the pane process group... nohup'd process ignores HUP. But there may be a race: systemd-run is a client that must talk to systemd BEFORE it can detach. If killed before establishing the unit, nothing happens. Also stdout/stderr redirected to /dev/null, fine.\n\nActually more precisely: when the popup's shell exits, tmux closes the pty. Any processes still holding the pty get SIGHUP. nohup makes systemd-run ignore SIGHUP... but wait, `nohup systemd-run ... &` — the & job is a child of the script shell. When shell exits, child is reparented, keeps running unless HUP. With nohup it ignores HUP. So systemd-run should survive...\n\nUnless: the timing issue — systemd-run hadn't even started yet (bash forks, execs nohup→systemd-run). Popup close happens microseconds after script exit. tmux might SIGKILL remaining processes? For regular panes tmux sends SIGHUP then closes. Hmm.\n\nBUT compare with plain-shell case: same backgrounding works because the terminal stays alive, giving systemd-run time to register the transient unit with systemd (which then owns the work).\n\nIn my earlier successful tests (m0035 test A), I ran systemd-run FOREGROUND from my long-lived shell. And in test B/C via tmux pane — those panes also stayed alive briefly (I slept 6-7s while pane command completed). rc=0 written after completion. Those didn't open tabs either per hyprctl... but user says example.com tabs DID open (they closed one!). Confusing. Maybe B/C/D/E all opened as BACKGROUND tabs (window title stays YouTube since tab opens unfocused?). Firefox opens URL in new foreground tab normally though... but zen with \"example.com\" — maybe opened in a new window on the same workspace BEHIND? Only 3 clients listed though.\n\nWhatever the visibility details, the KEY difference now proven by trace+journal: from the popup, the systemd-run line NEVER REACHES systemd (no journal entry at 16:0x). The process gets killed when the popup closes before it can talk to systemd.\n\nFix options:\n1. Don't rely on background+nohup: run systemd-run with `--unit` in FOREGROUND but detached via systemd itself: systemd-run returns immediately after creating the unit anyway (it's fast). Just call systemd-run without `&`/nohup — it registers unit and exits ~50ms. Popup stays open a few extra ms. \n2. Or use `setsid` instead of nohup to fully detach session.\n3. Or add tiny `sleep` after backgrounding.\n\nCleanest: drop `nohup ... &` entirely and let systemd-run run synchronously (it detaches the actual app into its own unit; the client exits immediately). Same for fallback branches (omarchy-launch-browser, gio, xdg-open) — keep nohup+& but add setsid to survive popup teardown? For gio/xdg-open they need to stay alive longer; setsid + redirect + disown. Use `setsid -f`? Actually simplest robust: replace `nohup X &` with `setsid X >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null &` plus small `sleep 0.05`? Hmm, still race-y.\n\nFor systemd-run path: synchronous call = guaranteed registration.\nFor other paths: use `setsid --fork X ...` which forks and parent exits immediately, child survives anything.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```bash\nif [[ \"$browser_desktop\" == ... ]]; then\n    systemd-run --user --quiet --collect \\\n        --unit=\"open-repo-browser-$(date +%s%N)\" \\\n        uwsm-app -- flatpak run app.zen_browser.zen \"$url\" \\\n        >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null\nelif omarchy-launch-browser; then\n    setsid --fork omarchy-launch-browser \"$url\" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null\n...\n```\n\nWait, does systemd-run client exit immediately? Yes — it creates the transient unit and returns; service runs under systemd. The previous author added nohup+& probably out of habit. Synchronous is fine and fast.\n\nAlso should verify why journal showed NOTHING even from the popup run — consistent with kill-before-registration.\n\nThen test through popup again and confirm journal entry + tab opens.",
